  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-117737",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect with a Line",
      "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ intersects the line $x+2y=4$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation $x+2y=4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2}x+2)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+4 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={2}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4-15 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-11<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $x+2y=4$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/400b997f9f1e83d6d2dd6c34b68a01ad4de5b352e40de2401eb516ca181f3163.png)",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "$-2$ and $4$",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
