  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14050050",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-7632",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse",
      "problem": "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is $5{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-10x-36y=4.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-10x-36y & =4 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}-10x)+(9{y}^{2}-36y) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10x)+(9{y}^{2}-36y) & =4 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2x]+9[{y}^{2}-4y] & =4 \\\\ 5[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+9[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =4 \\\\ 5[(x-1)^{2}-1]+9[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =4 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}-5+9(y-2)^{2}-36 & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x-1)^{2}-5+9(y-2)^{2}-36 & =4 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}+9(y-2)^{2} & =4+5+36 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}+9(y-2)^{2} & =45 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(1,2).$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
