    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-3397",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts",
      "problem": "$\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{4}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{13}{9}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
