    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-125926",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b839f19a-161c-5cb4-bc08-990f09afb900",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Logarithmic Function",
      "problem": "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x}).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, using the laws of logarithms, we observe that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ we recall the standard result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) & =x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo obtain the expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-4x),$ we replace $x$ with $-4x$ in the standard expansion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-4x) & =(-4x)-\\frac{(-4x)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(-4x)^{3}}{3}-⋯ \\\\  & =-4x-8{x}^{2}-\\frac{64{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, subtracting the expansion for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-4x)$ from the expansion for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-4x) \\\\  & =(x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯)-(-4x-8{x}^{2}-\\frac{64{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯) \\\\  & =(1+4)x+(-\\frac{1}{2}+8){x}^{2}+(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{64}{3}){x}^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =5x+\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{65{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
